{"kind":"artifact","text":"The gs status reference states the distinction in plain English, per ISO 24495-1, at the rebased head. A section, Two kinds of staleness, says that ordinary reasoning staleness means a basis under a record was retired so the reasoning moved while the record did not, and that it blocks nothing; and that a superseded world is narrower, means the described behaviour has been replaced, and stops a merge. It then says what the default view does with each: counts ordinary staleness per lane and marks no row, keeps finished commitments out of the lists whether or not they are stale, and keeps retired state words and superseded-world notes on their own rows with their own counts. It names the escape hatches — --all prints a stale qualifier per commitment, --json is untouched, and the bounded summary keeps its per-row stale field. Both illustrative figures were re-measured on the current projection after the #6356 and #6350 merges landed and corrected in the prose: the lane example now reads reported 27 (24 stale), and the artifact line reads 55 current, 121 stale, 1037 retired, 388 describing a superseded world. This satisfies condition 3. Tests passed: go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 (all four documentation gates, uncached after the edits) and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0.","body":{"branch":"request/staleness-presentation","commit":"0ff186ec952ee84dda6fadeeb04ea9316b0aec24","head":"0ff186ec952ee84dda6fadeeb04ea9316b0aec24","path":"docs/reference/gs/status.md"}}